 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 241920,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<figure class=\"unnumbered\">\n<img src=\"exercises/media/66c060a6fafb44d4.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<figcaption>(credit: modification of work by H. Maurice Goodman/ScienceDirect)</figcaption>\n</figure>",
   "stem_html": "<p>The antidiuretic hormone (ADH) controls the amount of water excreted by the kidneys. The graph shows the ADH level in the blood against blood osmolarity (osmomolality) in pregnant and nonpregnant women. Note higher osmomolarity means the blood has more salts and less water.</p>\n<p>Make a claim based on this graph.</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 757142,
     "content_html": "At the same osmomolarity, nonpregnant women secrete more ADH."
    },
    {
     "id": 757141,
     "content_html": "At the same osmomolarity, pregnant and nonpregnant women secrete the same amounts of ADH."
    },
    {
     "id": 757143,
     "content_html": "At the same ADH level in the blood, the blood of pregnant women has more water compared to the blood of nonpregnant women."
    },
    {
     "id": 757140,
     "content_html": "At the same ADH level in the blood, the blood of pregnant and nonpregnant women have equal concentrations of salt."
    }
   ],
